Let $(V,v)$ be an algebraic complex germ, let $S\subset V$ be a reduced closed subgerm, and suppose the analytic pair is $(\Delta^d\times T,\Delta^d\times\{t\})$. There are an algebraic transverse germ $(T_0,t_0)$ and common étale neighborhoods of pairs $$\label{str:etale-product-pair}
 (V,S)\ \longleftarrow\ (U,S_U)\ \longrightarrow\
 (\mathbb A^d\times T_0,\mathbb A^d\times\{t_0\}).$$ Both inverse-image ideals defining $S_U$ are equal, without truncation. For $k\geq1$, put $A_k=\mathcal O_{T_0,t_0}/\mathfrak m_{t_0}^k$ and let $U_k$ be defined by the $k$th power of this common ideal. After shrinking the displayed neighborhoods, there are compatible isomorphisms $$\label{str:finite-product}
 U_k\simeq S_U\times\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}A_k
 \quad\text{over}\quad \mathbb A^d\times\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}A_k.$$ No equivariance of these product isomorphisms is asserted.

Let $L$ be a complete noetherian normal local domain, and let $A_1,A_2$ be finite dominant local $L$-algebras that are regular local rings. Put $d_i=\mathop{\mathrm{rk}}_L A_i$. If these rings have positive characteristic and arise by completing local rings of varieties over a perfect field, then $$\label{bc:regular-comparison}
                 A_1^{\oplus d_2}\simeq A_2^{\oplus d_1}
                 \qquad\text{as $L$-modules}.$$ The same conclusion holds in characteristic zero when the diagram is the completion of an algebraic finite diagram at a closed point over $\mathbb C$. Finite products of the indicated local algebras can also be compared, using their total generic ranks.

Let $R$ be noetherian, let $\mathsf H$ be an algebra finite over its central subring $R$, and let $\mathcal P$ on $Y=\mathbb P^n_R$ be locally finite projective as a right $\mathsf H_Y$-module. There is an integer $m_0$ such that, for every $m\ge m_0$ and every left $\mathsf H$-module $Q$, $$\begin{aligned}
 H^j(Y,\mathcal P(m)\otimes_{\mathsf H}Q)&=0
                     &&(j>0),\label{bc:uniform-vanishing}\\
 H^0(Y,\mathcal P(m))\otimes_{\mathsf H}Q
       &\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }
 H^0(Y,\mathcal P(m)\otimes_{\mathsf H}Q).
                     \label{bc:uniform-sections}
\end{aligned}$$ The arrow is the canonical multiplication map, and $H^0(Y,\mathcal P(m))$ is a finite projective right $\mathsf H$-module. The bound is independent of $Q$.

For the fixed $g\ne0$ in <a href="#bc:cleared-homotopy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="bc:cleared-homotopy">[bc:cleared-homotopy]</a>, on a finite principal-open cover of $P$ there are finitely many $t_j\in R$ and algebraic differential operators $D_j$ with regular coefficients on the respective open sets such that $$\label{bc:differential-equation}
                  \eta=\sum_j D_j(g t_j\eta).$$ The identity is in the middle relative de Rham module on the generic smooth locus, but its operators are regular also at the other points of the chosen open sets. For one integer $o_g$ fixed in characteristic zero, they have expressions $$\label{bc:operator-bound}
 D_j=\sum_{|\alpha|\le o_g}c_{j,\alpha}(u)\partial_u^\alpha,
 \qquad
 \partial_u^\alpha=
 \partial_{u_1}^{\alpha_1}\cdots\partial_{u_n}^{\alpha_n},$$ where each $c_{j,\alpha}$ is regular on its specified open set.

There are arbitrarily large remaining residue characteristics, at degree-one places of a finite extension of the number field, for which $$\operatorname{Car}(F_*\sigma_X)\ne0.$$ On the geometric special fiber, the map $$\label{bc:cartier-splitting}
 \psi_X:F_*\mathcal O_X\longrightarrow\mathcal O_X,
 \qquad \psi_X(z)=\operatorname{Car}(F_*(z\sigma_X))/\sigma_X$$ satisfies $\psi_X(1)=\delta\in k^*$.

In the barred tensor calculus, the trivial-isotypic multiplicity bundle of $V=N_1$ is canonically $$\mathcal U=\overline{\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}}(\mathcal O,V)=\Omega^1_{\mathcal S}.$$ Let $W$ be the complementary object to this evaluation summand. There are idempotents $p_b\in\overline{\mathcal E}$, supported in the $N_b$ block, whose images are $\Lambda^bW$. If $L$ is any left $\overline{\mathcal E}$-module, then $$\label{eu:block-exterior}
 \mathbf1_{N_i}L\simeq
 \bigoplus_{a+b=i}\Lambda^a\Omega^1_{\mathcal S}\otimes p_bL.$$ Moreover, there are an integer $j_0$ and an isotypic idempotent $u$ in the $N_{j_0}$ block such that $$\label{eu:isolated-constituent}
 u\overline{\mathcal E}p_b=
 \begin{cases}
   \mathcal O_{\mathcal S}u,&b=j_0,\\
   0,&b\ne j_0.
 \end{cases}$$ The identity line in this formula has trivial action under every stabilizer.

For one sufficiently large integer $k$, set $$\mathcal S_k=(\widehat{\mathcal B},\mathcal O/I^k),\quad
 T_i=N_i/I^kN_i,\quad T=\bigoplus_iT_i.$$ There is a canonical surjection $$\label{eu:full-end-quotient}
 \mathop{\mathrm{End}}_{\mathcal S_k}(T)\longrightarrow\overline{\mathcal E}$$ with nilpotent kernel, compatible with the action of $\mathcal E$. After specializing any smooth product parameters, the image of ambient endomorphisms in the specialized $\mathop{\mathrm{End}}(T)$ still surjects onto the specialized barred algebra, with nilpotent kernel.

On a fixed good reduction, every product module $M$ satisfies $$\label{eu:test-leading-zero}
           \int_X\mathop{\mathrm{ch}}_c(f^*M)\mathop{\mathrm{td}}_{2n-c}(X)=0.$$ Consequently $$\label{eu:all-tests-zero}
 \chi(X,f^*M)=
 \chi\bigl(\mathcal S,\lambda_{-1}(\Omega^1_{\mathcal S})\otimes\gamma(M)\bigr)=0.$$

There exist vector bundles $P_i$ on $\mathcal S_k$ with surjections $P_i\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal A_i$. For their evaluation map $$\label{eu:evaluation}
 Y_0=\bigoplus_iT_i\otimes P_i
       \xrightarrow{\ \Phi\ }T_{j_0},\qquad Z_0=\ker\Phi,$$ the source, target, kernel, image and cokernel are product modules. The kernel sequence remains exact after setting the smooth product parameters to zero, including when those parameters have been chosen equivariantly for a point stabilizer.

At each geometric point of $\mathcal S$, in the representation ring of its full stabilizer, $$\label{eu:trivial-fiber}
 \gamma(M_{T_{j_0}})-\gamma(M_{Y_0})+\gamma(M_{Z_0})
                    =(-1)^{j_0}[\mathbf1].$$

Let $D\subset B$ be a prime divisor, let $E\subset T$ be a prime divisor over $D$, and put $D_A=a(E)$. Write $$M=e_E(p),\qquad L=e_E(a)$$ for the ramification indices of the two finite maps, and let $m_D$ be the fiber multiplicity in Lemma <a href="#iso:vertical-divisor" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="iso:vertical-divisor">55</a>. Then $$\label{iso:multiplicity-bound}
                             Lm_D\le M.$$ The linear part of a generator of the cyclic inertia is either the identity or a pseudoreflection, on both $A$ and $P$. If $M_l,L_l$ are the analogous indices for $p_l,a_l$, then $$\label{iso:linear-ramification-bound}
                              L_l\le M_l.$$

In $K^0(\mathfrak B)$, $$\label{quot:direct-image}
       \widetilde f_![\mathcal O_X]
       =\sum_{j=0}^{n}(-1)^j[\Omega^j_{\mathfrak B}]
       =\lambda_{-1}(\Omega^1_{\mathfrak B}).$$ Moreover, for some integer $m>0$, $$\label{quot:nilpotence}
  \bigl([T_{\mathfrak B}]-n[\mathcal O_{\mathfrak B}]\bigr)^m
                \lambda_{-1}(\Omega^1_{\mathfrak B})=0 .$$
